WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
this is tumor hussein reading for  preview of randolph ramsay metroid red  doesn't waste any time in introducing  what exactly there is to tread in the  long awaited sequel many of the game's  trailers so far have featured the long  limbed quadrupedal robots known as emmys  who seem to be near impossible to kill  sentries that series protagonist samus  aran must simply avoid emmys or at least  one version of them are there right from  the start of your adventure and during  my recent hands-on playthrough of the  game's opening sector emmys proved to be  an effective compelling way of  instilling a healthy amount of panic to  the series's tried and true core of  action and exploration granted the last  game in this specific metroid series  metroid fusion which was released a  whopping 19 years ago featured a similar  nemesis-like unstoppable enemy in the  sax but while the sax was merely a  chilling presence in limited and  scripted spurts the emmys in dread  looked like there will be a much more  present and constant threat in my  hour-long playthrough the winding path  of progress took me through enemy  patrolled areas several times which  meant repeated cat and mouse-like  encounters although my time with the  game was brief it was enough for me to  feel twinges of anxiety every time i saw  one of the telltale shimmering black and  white doors that indicated an emmy was  somewhere on the other side  [Music]  but let's take it back a step and frame  where metroid dread takes place within  the wider series dread is officially the  fifth game in what nintendo terms as the  2d saga which started with the original  metroid on the famicom way back in 1986  or 1987 for the nes the series which  also includes metroid 2 super metroid  and metroid fusion charts the story of  bounty hunter samus aran and her  seemingly intertwined fate with the  species known as metroid as well as her  encounters with a parasitic lifeform  known simply as x a whole bunch of space  pirates and a mysterious extinct or are  they civilization known as the chozo  it's a lot to remember especially as  this story has taken 35 years to unfold  it's no wonder that metroid dread makes  sure to catch you up on the narrative so  far with a brief recap right at the  start of the game the intro gives some  necessary background along with a  healthy dose of mystery the dangerous x  parasite thought to have been wiped out  after the events of metroid fusion have  been spotted again on the planet zdr the  galactic federation sent a team of  robotic emmys to search for the x but  they have been mysteriously corrupted  now it's up to the hyper-capable samus  iran to figure out exactly what happened  to the emmys and to confirm if x is  again a credible threat adding to the  mystery is an unidentified chozo warrior  who promptly whops samus's butt the  minute she lands on zdr stripping away  most of her abilities before abandoning  her at the bottom of zdr's extensive  tunnel system and yes that means that in  a break from the usual metroid tradition  samus has to work her way up instead of  down during the game despite the forced  ability reset samus is still a sprightly  bounty hunter at the start of dread and  controlling her as she moves around the  opening sector of actaria is a breeze  her new ability to slide under low  obstacles makes early movements  especially zippy this mobility is her  key defense against the aforementioned  emmys while the first emmy you'll  encounter is broken and only serves as a  tutorial showing how emmys can be  destroyed once you get a rare power up  the second is much more ominous it's  fully mobile moves quickly can scale up  walls and ceilings and is totally  impervious to samus's weapons in metroid  red it seems that enemies will be  limited to certain areas within each  sector emmys patrol their designated  zones sending out sonic pings visualized  by yellow circles on the screen to  detect sound or movement once they  detect something they'll quickly  investigate and if you're spotted it's a  relentless chase until they lose sight  of you during my playthrough i had to  continuously traverse emmy patrol zones  as i looked for the way out which in  typical metroid fashion involved  detailed exploration and eventually  earning new abilities to find ways to  reach what seemed like inaccessible  areas going into emmy areas for me were  initially frenetic bought for the exit  affairs with any mistimed jumps or  missed ledges ending in quick emmy  assisted death but that frantic feeling  eventually subsided into a more  controlled tension as the game forced  more emmy interactions in one area i had  to stand on a switch for a certain  amount of time in order to access the  next room this simple task was made  complicated by a pursuing emmy which  meant i had to get enough distance away  from the robot stand on the switch for  as long as i could before bolting and  leading the emmy away so i could return  to the switch again it was thrilling  despite their apparent indestructibility  there are ways to get away from an emmy  and it's another area where dread  manages to ably ratchet up the tension  you can if you're quick enough counter  an enemy attack once it has you in its  grasp much like in metroid samus returns  most enemies i encountered in metroid  dread could be counted just before they  attacked but the window for countering  emmys was especially tiny out of perhaps  the seven times i was caught during my  brief playtime i only managed to counter  once and even then it seemed more like  luck than skill on my part you'll also  be able to completely destroy an emmy in  a sector once you find and defeat that  sector's main control unit and grab a  single use of mega cannon powerup when  you take on an enemy with this power up  your point of view shifts to a close  behind the back view of samus from where  you can aim your fire directly at the  robot's armored head it takes quite a  few shots to first blow away the armor  and then deliver the finishing blow and  the act of holding my nerve and shooting  an enemy as it slowly advanced towards  me was quite a rush  by the time my hands on ended i had  encountered a major boss fight and  earned a new ability that allowed me to  move up certain wars but was those tense  emmy chases that stayed with me well  after my play time it also made me  wonder how emmys will evolve as the game  progresses as previous dread trailers  have shown off different colored emmys  which means this game's most intriguing  enemies very likely have more surprises  the metroid games have always excelled  at creating an underlying mood of unease  and in metroid red it's clear that  unease is meant to boil over into a  persistent constant tension  you

